  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining Our Focus**\n\nI\'m solidifying the design elements, ensuring a clean aesthetic and dark mode compatibility. The PWA integration is locked down. Next, I\'ll integrate the Google Maps element.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Leaflet Integration**\n\nI\'ve shifted focus to Leaflet and OpenStreetMap, as the Google Maps Platform is no longer needed. Installing `leaflet` and `@types/leaflet` is underway, as is the development of the interactive map component. I\'m ensuring it meets requirements, including a draggable pin and radius circle, using OSM tiles. I\'ve also verified the Firebase config and will now review the home page requirements.\n\n\n**Defining User Flows**\n\nI\'m now outlining the user interactions for marking. \"Scan QR\" and \"Enter ID\" buttons will lead to different tabs within the `/mark` route, ensuring proper redirection via `ProtectedRoute` and `LoginPage` for unauthenticated users. I\'m focusing on preserving query parameters during login redirects. Also, the unique ID generation logic is planned.\n\n\n**Generating Unique IDs**\n\nI\'m implementing a robust ID generation system for all user roles (Admin, Manager, Member). I\'m focusing on atomic Firestore transactions to reserve these 6-character IDs. I\'ve designed retries with random digits and letter cycling to guarantee uniqueness. The `orgService`, `userService`, and `demoService` are being updated to leverage this new system and ensure proper reservation and cleanup. I\'m also updating QR codes to encode the new data format. I\'m moving on to implement the live rotating QR session.\n\n\n**Designing Session Initiation**\n\nI\'m now detailing session initiation workflows, differentiating roles: Admins start for Managers, and Managers for Members. I\'ve designed an Attendance Setup form with session title, duration, allowed radius (with default values, a slider, and a Leaflet map). It features a draggable pin and radius circle with location data. A link to Manual Attendance is included.\n\n\n**Implementing Live QR Session**\n\nI\'m now implementing the live rotating QR session. The system will automatically acquire the screen during a session, refreshing on visibility changes, and releasing on unmount. A clear QR code, generated by `QRCodeSVG` or `QRCodeCanvas`, will refresh every 10 seconds with a smooth SVG countdown ring. Alongside this, a live 6-digit code will rotate on the same 10-second tick. A real-time counter will display the marked count vs. the active subordinate count. There will also be a list of names as people mark, including ID, time, distance, and status (Present/Late). We\'ll also see rejected attempts, with reasons, in real-time. Finally, I will integrate \"Close session\" and \"Extend 10 min\" buttons.\n\n\n**Defining Session Mechanisms**\n\nI\'m now detailing the mechanics of the session token rotation, which will refresh every 10 seconds. The starter\'s device will update `sessionSecrets/{sessionId}`, publishing a new token, code, and timestamps. Both current and previous token/code combinations will be valid for short periods to cover latency. I\'m focusing on the member/manager side to mark attendance, using `/mark`, and I will automatically find the active session.\n\n\n**Evaluating Attendance Process**\n\nI\'m now detailing the attendance process, with Step 1 being an automatic GPS check, and Step 2 using either a QR scan or manual ID entry. I\'m focusing on providing clear feedback, including accuracy checks and distance computations. I\'ll make sure to display status cleanly, even when the `org.locationCheckEnabled` setting is off. I\'m also planning the submission process, and will include local validation, ensuring active session and ID matching.\n\n\n**Validating Attendance Submissions**\n\nI\'m now implementing the validation process for attendance. My focus is on ensuring data integrity and security with Firebase rules. I will check GPS accuracy and radius, and write to both `attendance` and `sessionDevices` in a single batch, with validations in the rules. Rejection informs the user with \"Already marked today\" or \"invalid token/code\". I\'m also ensuring the check for existing data.\n\n\n**Finalizing User Feedback**\n\nI\'m now detailing the user feedback for successful and failed attempts. For failures, I\'m differentiating between various reasons like closed sessions or expired QR codes with a clear message and a \"QR expired, scan again\" prompt. I\'m focusing on ensuring accurate and informative error messages to the user. For success, the green \"Attendance marked\" confirmation card is the final step.\n\n\n**Verifying Rule Integrity**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into Firestore security rules. I\'m ensuring all expressions compile and execute flawlessly in Firebase Rules v2, particularly focusing on `math.sqrt` and `math.pow` functions. The flat-earth approximation distance calculation is being verified, including the `org.locationCheckEnabled` switch, ensuring location checks are honored correctly. I\'m also confirming the token/code verification mechanism.\n\n\n**Validating Access Logic**\n\nI\'m now integrating logic for attendance method validation within Firestore rules, focusing on secure time-based token/code checks. I\'m verifying the current and previous token validity within specified timeframes, and ensuring the submitted data matches the correct token or code. The final validation steps involve confirming the user\'s ID matches the authenticated user.\n\n\n**Analyzing Access Permissions**\n\nI\'m now deep in Firestore security rules, verifying session access. I\'m focusing on user roles and relationships, including admin/manager/member hierarchies, and the verification of sessions being open and not expired. The document ID check and related constraints are also being meticulously reviewed for integrity. My checks ensure a new record\'s uniqueness, preventing duplicates, and confirming device existence, which requires a check for the session devices\' entry.\n\n\n\n**Securing Device Validation**\n\nI\'m now refining device-specific validations within the Firestore security rules to enhance access control. I\'m focusing on preventing device reuse in a session with these new rules: `deviceId && request.resource.data.personUid == request.auth.uid && !exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/sessionDevices/$(devDocId))` to ensure the device is not yet used and validating `getAfter(/databases/$(database)/documents/sessionDevices/$(sid + \'_\' + request.resource.data.deviceId)).data.personUid == request.auth.uid`. Also, I\'m ensuring data integrity with atomicity.\n\n\n\n**Securing Access Control**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the Firestore security rules. Specifically, I\'m addressing an issue related to the `sessionDevices` document. I\'m ensuring device-specific validations are rock-solid, preventing re-use within a session. I\'m verifying the rules for `userIds` and `attempts` collections, also verifying access to sessions.\n\n\n**Verifying Access Rules**\n\nI\'m now deep in Firestore security rule verification for the new `sessions` and `sessionSecrets` documents. I\'m focusing on validating access control logic for session owners. Also, I\'m checking session creation rules, ensuring data integrity across both documents. Specifically, I\'m confirming that new sessions can only be created by admins or managers within the correct org.\n\n\n**Implementing Security Features**\n\nI\'m now implementing the access control logic for `sessionSecrets`, securing updates and enforcing role-based permissions. My focus is ensuring only the session owner, either an admin or a manager, can read, create, update, or delete session secrets, and ensuring that the owner can be determined with `resource.data.ownerUid == request.auth.uid`. Also, the introduction of a new highlight for the manual entries.\n\n**Enhancing Reporting and Settings**\n\nI\'m now working to enhance the reporting and settings functionalities. Manual attendance entries, marked by `method === \'manual\'`, will be visually distinct.  I\'ll also integrate the 6-character ID throughout reports and exports. In organization settings, I\'m adding a customizable default radius, which will support a meter range from 20 to 500, with 50 as default.\n\n\n"
