  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Identifying Key Gaps**\n\nI\'ve just finished reviewing the current implementation against the user\'s updated requirements. It\'s become clear that there are some significant gaps. I need to be precise about how to make necessary upgrades and avoid breaking existing functionality. The solution will require careful planning and a targeted approach.\n\n\n**Analyzing Requirement Adherence**\n\nI\'m now deeply immersed in a side-by-side comparison. I\'m rigorously cross-referencing user requests with the present code. Specifically, the urgent issue of the 10-second QR token expiration and prior token rejection requires immediate attention. It\'s imperative that I pinpoint the exact code modifications necessary to enforce backend validation and prevent any manipulation of QR payloads, which is critical for security.\n\n\n**Addressing Security Vulnerabilities**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the core security vulnerabilities, specifically the window of vulnerability concerning QR code expiration. My current focus centers on implementing a failsafe to reject previous QR codes after the 10-second window. I\'m exploring storing server-verified timestamps. Immediate invalidation upon rotation is key. Active status checks and enforcing strict validation in Firestore rules are also high priorities.\n\n\n**Refining Authorization Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the authorization logic to address potential clock skew and write latency during token rotation. To handle this, on rotation I\'ll be writing the new token, code, active status, `tokenUpdatedAt`, and a `rotationSeq` to `sessionSecrets/{sessionId}` FIRST. Then, only after the `await updateDoc(...)` resolves will I update the displayed QR. This guarantees data consistency and prevents invalid token acceptance.\n\n\n**Implementing Atomic Updates**\n\nI\'m now focusing on atomic updates to enhance session security and attendance integrity. Specifically, I\'m ensuring that a user\'s attendance status and session details are updated atomically. This includes updating `sessionSecrets/{sessionId}` with `active = false`, and invalidating the QR code simultaneously. This prevents race conditions and ensures immediate invalidation of any active QR tokens on the backend when a session ends.\n\n\n**Preventing Duplicate Entries**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on duplicate entry prevention. The current attendance record structure, `${orgId}_${date}_${personUid}`, presents a subtle bug that managers could exploit with repeated scans. To resolve, I\'m analyzing Firestore\'s `batch.set()` behavior and security rules. My approach aims to implement atomic, per-session, per-user duplicate prevention, even with concurrent live sessions and retaining compatibility with the current data structure.\n\n\n**Securing Atomic Operations**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on duplicate entry prevention. The current system relies on various queries including `orgId`, `date`, `managerId`, `personUid`, and `sessionId`. The current attendance record structure, `${orgId}_${date}_${personUid}`, introduces a potential risk of exploitation. I\'m exploring Firestore\'s transaction features, focusing on `runTransaction` to implement atomic, per-session, per-user duplicate prevention. Specifically, I\'m verifying session status and ensuring the absence of an existing attendance record within the transaction. I will perform all reads and writes in a single, atomic operation to eliminate race conditions.\n\n\n**Validating Data Consistency**\n\nI\'ve just revisited Test #9 and its implications. To address the potential for multiple users scanning the same QR code, I\'m now incorporating rigorous validation steps. First, I\'ll read the `attendance/{recordId}`. If the record exists, I\'ll throw an `ALREADY_MARKED` error, preventing duplicates. Then, I\'ll read `sessionDevices/{sessionId}_{deviceId}`. If a record exists and the `personUid` doesn\'t match the current user, a `DEVICE_USED` error will be thrown. Finally, atomic transactions via `tx.set()` will be used to ensure `sessionDeviceRef` and `attendanceRef` updates are consistent. The backend\'s Firestore rules will be updated to validate everything, including the server-side checks and permissions, preventing overwriting of existing records.\n\n\n**Refining Concurrent User Handling**\n\nI\'m now investigating a scenario involving concurrent QR code scans and device identity. Specifically, I realized that multiple users could potentially scan the same active QR code simultaneously if they are using different devices. However, I\'ve now clarified that, while this is permissible, there is an existing mechanism to prevent a single device from being used to represent multiple users during the same session. I am also investigating to see how `Test #9` applies to this understanding, and if any updates are needed.\n\n\n\n**Enhancing Live Session UI**\n\nI\'m now focused on the live session UI, specifically the `StartAttendancePage.tsx`. I\'ve confirmed the existing QR code and 10-second countdown timer implementation, but now I\'m adding a prominent, unmistakable \"LIVE\" status indicator with a pulsing red/emerald dot and text, directly next to the session title. I\'ll also incorporate clearer session details like the Session ID, target role, organizer name, start time, remaining time, and radius for enhanced clarity.\n\n\n**Clarifying Session Control**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the session\'s \"Close Session\" functionality. I\'ll make sure it immediately stops QR code rotation by clearing the interval, resetting the `currentToken` and `currentCode` in memory, and updating `sessionSecrets` with `active: false` and `sessions` with `status: \'closed\'`. I\'m also reviewing the bug in `StartAttendancePage.tsx`, specifically lines 209-255, where `activeSession?.endsAt`\'s object reference changes on every Firestore snapshot, triggering unnecessary re-renders.\n\n\n**Optimizing Interval Accuracy**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on optimizing the 10-second interval accuracy and addressing the potential for timer throttling across browser tabs. Instead of relying on a `Timestamp` object, I\'m refactoring to depend on only `activeSession?.id` and read `endsAtMillisRef.current`. I\'m also now tracking the `tokenIssuedAtMsRef.current` timestamp during QR rotation and calculating `elapsedSec` dynamically on each tick to ensure accurate expiration, and to immediately trigger a rotation when necessary, even if the tab is throttled. This guarantees the QR code is always valid, no matter the browser state.\n\n\n**Improving UI/UX Synchronization**\n\nI\'m now focusing on synchronizing the countdown with wall-clock time after token issuance in Firestore. This ensures accurate expiration, no matter the browser state. To prevent overlapping `rotateSessionSecret` calls, I\'m using an `isRotatingRef` lock and an `aborted` flag. This ensures QR token generation stops immediately when the session ends. I\'m also examining the scenario when the Admin/Manager starts or stops the Live Session after the Teacher/Member opens the Scanner screen. I\'m looking at `MarkYourAttendancePage.tsx` to refresh the UI accurately and prevent manual \"Check again\" clicks, and to make sure the scanner UI is up-to-date even when sessions end.\n\n\n**Refining UI Update Mechanism**\n\nI\'m now implementing real-time updates to the scanner UI using a Firestore listener for active sessions. The moment an admin starts a live session, the scanner will update automatically, displaying live status and session details. When the session ends, the scanner UI will reflect the change immediately. I\'m also addressing the edge case where a user attempts to scan a QR code from a previously ended or unauthorized session, to ensure the UI is in a correct, consistent state.\n\n\n\n**Refining Scanner UI Logic**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the scanner UI updates, especially when a user scans a QR code that has expired, or is from an inactive session, or is from an unauthorized session. Instead of automatically hiding the scanner, I\'ll display a session-specific error message directly. Specifically, if a scanner scans an ended session\'s QR code, it will show an immediate `\"Session has ended\"` error message, while still displaying the QR scanner. If there is no active parent session, I\'ll show a calm banner, but still enable the user to try scanning codes.\n\n**Improving Scanner UI**\n\nI\'m now focusing on scanner UI improvements based on requirement 6, specifically the scanning frame and desktop compatibility. I\'ll be adding a corner-bracket scanning frame overlay and adding \"Verifying...\" feedback to the user. I\'ll handle camera permissions and error notifications, and ensure mobile responsiveness. A key update addresses the desktop compatibility issue with `facingMode: \'environment\'`, automatically falling back to any available webcam on desktop if the back camera is unavailable.\n\n\n\n**Enhancing Scanner Navigation**\n\nI\'m now integrating a dedicated \"QR Scanner\" option for managers. I\'m modifying the `ManagerDashboard.tsx` to include a prominent \"Scan QR\" button with a QR code icon, linking to the `/mark?tab=scan` route. This is in line with the requirement to provide clear scanner access to users with appropriate lower-level authority. This mirrors the existing scanner functionality present in the MemberDashboard.\n\n\n\n**Validating Server-Side Data**\n\nI\'m now focused on data validation to make the data server-side secure. I\'ve ensured a 10-second token validity window using the server clock. Token forgery and session manipulation are impossible, and I\'m validating every write against multiple checks. Server rules ensure orgId, role, managerId, request time and other checks are valid, and session devices are atomic. I\'m focusing on strengthening the integrity of the data.\n\n\n**Validating Security Measures**\n\nI\'m now emphasizing the inherent security of the current Firestore setup, particularly its validation mechanisms. Server-side validation via Firestore Security Rules renders client-side manipulations ineffective. Existing checks against `request.time`, `request.auth`, and session data, including those in the `runTransaction` are sufficient. The free Spark plan\'s limitations are not a security issue, as Cloud Functions are not needed.\n\n\n**Verifying Server-Side Enforcement**\n\nI\'m now ensuring the Firestore rules enforce *all* security invariants rigorously, rejecting writes with `PERMISSION_DENIED` in cases of invalid data, or multiple sessions on the same day. Specifically, I\'m verifying the backend *always* rejects submissions due to payload errors, wrong orgId, incorrect role, closed sessions, and duplicate attendance. The goal is zero client-only checks. The current `attendance` document ID structure `${orgId}_${date}_${personUid}` means only one record per user per day is allowed. The current `submitSelfAttendance` throws an `errAlreadyMarked` error message if the person has already been marked for attendance on the given day. I\'m focusing on strengthening the integrity of the data.\n\n\n**Confirming Data Integrity**\n\nI\'m now focusing on data integrity. I\'m confirming that the current `attendance` collection, with its deterministic document ID structure (`${orgId}_${date}_${personUid}`), already prevents duplicate entries per user per session, satisfying Requirement 7. I\'m also planning to check if the user is already marked for this session as soon as the session loads.\n\n\n**Confirming Attendance Logic**\n\nI\'m now confirming the logic around attendance records and security rules. If a user scans a QR code, the system now clearly shows an \"Already marked\" error if the user has already attended for the day. Unauthorized users are blocked both by client-side and server-side checks and rules. The server-side checks are crucial for maintaining data integrity and enforcing session permissions. Also, I\'m checking the current session to show the clear \"Already marked\" error immediately after load.\n\n\n**Verifying Access Control**\n\nI\'m now focused on validating access control, particularly when a user attempts to scan a QR code from a different organization or an inactive session. My analysis reveals that incorrect QR code scans are correctly rejected by Firestore rules due to payload errors and unauthorized session access. I am also checking how the `MarkYourAttendancePage` and `submitSelfAttendance` handle scans from different sessions. I\'ll need to confirm that `Inactive` or `pending` users are blocked by `isActive()`. I\'m verifying the backend *always* rejects submissions. I\'m focusing on the data integrity and security checks.\n\n\n**Refining Error Messaging**\n\nI\'m now refining the error messaging strategy for clarity and user experience, addressing every edge case from the provided scenarios. I will ensure a clean error messages. I\'m verifying that the system provides specific, accurate error messages. I\'m updating the `src/i18n/en.json` and `src/i18n/hi.json` files to include any new or revised keys, ensuring the translated content is synchronized across both languages.\n\n\n"
