steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1795106430
      nanos: 982086649
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
    source_trajectory_step_info {
      trajectory_id: "d7fd655d-a0ed-43d2-8085-8745e7e76a42"
      step_index: 184
      cascade_id: "d7fd655d-a0ed-43d2-8085-8745e7e76a42"
    }
    model_api_content_id: "db0c200af5da4fe5a90965b8649b51a7"
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "# ROLE: Senior Software Architect, Full-Stack Developer & UI/UX Engineer\n\nAct as a Senior Software Architect and Full-Stack Developer. Your task is to upgrade my existing GitHub project into a professional, production-quality Smart Campus Attendance Management System.\n\n**Existing GitHub Repository:**\nhttps://github.com/pankajadhikari820/mini-attendence.git\n\nFirst, thoroughly inspect the entire existing codebase, including frontend, backend, Firebase configuration, Firestore collections, authentication, security rules, user roles, and existing attendance workflows.\n\nDo not blindly rewrite the project. Understand the existing architecture before making changes.\n\n# 1. CRITICAL: Remove Late Attendance Completely\n\nCompletely eliminate the Late Attendance feature from the entire project.\n\nThe system must support ONLY these three attendance statuses:\n\n- PRESENT\n- ABSENT\n- LEAVE\n\nRequirements:\n- Remove LATE status from all frontend components.\n- Remove late attendance calculations and conditions.\n- Remove late-related dashboard statistics.\n- Remove late-related reports, filters, labels, and notifications.\n- Update Firestore-related logic and validation wherever necessary.\n- Ensure no legacy code can generate or display a LATE status.\n\nDo not introduce any additional attendance status.\n\n# 2. Digital Student ID Card\n\nCreate a professional digital student identity card.\n\nFeatures:\n- Student name and photograph.\n- Unique student ID / enrollment number.\n- Department and course.\n- Semester and section.\n- College name and logo.\n- Unique QR code linked to the student\'s authenticated identity.\n- Downloadable digital ID card in PDF format.\n\nEnsure the ID QR code cannot independently authorize attendance.\n\n# 3. Advanced Attendance Analytics\n\nCreate a modern analytics dashboard with:\n\n- Weekly and monthly attendance graphs.\n- Overall attendance percentage.\n- Subject-wise attendance.\n- PRESENT, ABSENT, and LEAVE statistics.\n- Attendance trends.\n- Department-wise and class-wise analytics.\n- Date range filters.\n- Interactive charts and downloadable reports.\n\n# 4. Low Attendance Alert System\n\nImplement an automated attendance monitoring system.\n\nRequirements:\n- Set 75% as the default attendance threshold.\n- Automatically identify students whose attendance percentage falls below 75%.\n- Notify students and authorized faculty.\n- Display low-attendance warnings on dashboards.\n- Show subject-wise attendance shortage.\n- Avoid duplicate notifications.\n- Allow authorized administrators to configure the threshold.\n\n# 5. Parent Portal\n\nCreate a separate, secure parent dashboard.\n\nFeatures:\n- Parent login.\n- Link parent accounts with verified student accounts.\n- View child\'s attendance percentage.\n- View subject-wise attendance.\n- View attendance history.\n- View leave applications and approval status.\n- Download attendance reports.\n- Receive low-attendance alerts.\n\nSecurity:\n- Parents must only access their linked child\'s information.\n- Parent accounts must not have permission to modify attendance.\n- Implement proper account verification and access control.\n\n# 6. Subject-wise and Classroom Attendance\n\nUpgrade the existing attendance workflow.\n\nFeatures:\n- Department management.\n- Course management.\n- Semester management.\n- Section management.\n- Classroom management.\n- Subject management.\n- Faculty-to-subject assignment.\n- Faculty-to-class assignment.\n- Subject-specific attendance sessions.\n- Student enrollment in subjects.\n\nFaculty should only be able to create and manage attendance sessions for their assigned classes and subjects.\n\n# 7. Complete Audit Log System\n\nImplement immutable audit logs for important system actions.\n\nTrack:\n- Attendance creation.\n- Attendance modification.\n- Attendance correction.\n- Leave approval and rejection.\n- Student account changes.\n- Faculty actions.\n- Role and permission changes.\n- Administrative actions.\n\nEach audit record should include:\n- User ID.\n- User role.\n- Action performed.\n- Target record.\n- Timestamp.\n- Relevant metadata.\n\nPrevent normal users and administrators from editing or deleting audit history. Use trusted backend operations and appropriate security rules.\n\n# 8. Smart Timetable Integration\n\nBuild a timetable management system.\n\nFeatures:\n- Weekly timetable.\n- Subject scheduling.\n- Faculty scheduling.\n- Classroom allocation.\n- Automatic attendance session creation.\n- Session start and end times.\n- Conflict detection.\n- Automatic reminders for faculty.\n- Prevent duplicate sessions.\n\nAttendance sessions must follow the configured timetable and authorized faculty assignments.\n\n# 9. Student Performance Dashboard\n\nCreate a personalized student dashboard.\n\nDisplay:\n- Overall attendance percentage.\n- Subject-wise attendance.\n- Weekly attendance trends.\n- Monthly progress.\n- Attendance shortage warnings.\n- Recent attendance history.\n- Leave history.\n- Upcoming timetable.\n\nUse clean charts, statistics, and modern visual components.\n\n# 10. Professional Excel/PDF Reporting\n\nImplement report generation for:\n\n- Individual students.\n- Individual subjects.\n- Classes and sections.\n- Departments.\n- Monthly attendance.\n- Date-range attendance.\n- PRESENT, ABSENT, and LEAVE summaries.\n\nSupport:\n- Excel export.\n- Professional PDF export.\n- College branding.\n- Generation timestamps.\n- Proper report formatting.\n\n# 11. Role-Based Access Control\n\nMaintain and strengthen the existing role hierarchy.\n\nSupport:\n- Super Admin.\n- Organization Admin.\n- Manager / Faculty.\n- Student.\n- Parent.\n\nEnforce permissions at the backend and Firestore Security Rules level.\n\nDo not rely only on hiding frontend buttons.\n\n# 12. UI/UX Modernization\n\nUpgrade the complete application interface to a premium SaaS-level design.\n\nDesign direction:\n- Modern professional dashboard.\n- Clean typography.\n- Consistent spacing.\n- Responsive layouts.\n- Professional sidebar navigation.\n- Attractive analytics cards.\n- Interactive graphs.\n- Smooth but subtle animations.\n- Consistent design system.\n- Light and dark modes.\n- Mobile-friendly student and parent dashboards.\n\nAvoid excessive gradients, unnecessary animations, and cluttered layouts.\n\nMaintain the existing college identity and branding wherever available.\n\n# 13. Firebase Architecture and Security\n\nPreserve the existing Firebase architecture wherever practical.\n\nReview and improve:\n- Firebase Authentication.\n- Cloud Firestore.\n- Firestore Security Rules.\n- Firebase Storage.\n- Cloud Functions, if available and required.\n- Data validation.\n- Role-based permissions.\n- Database indexes.\n- Secure API operations.\n\nImportant:\n- Never expose Firebase Admin credentials or private API keys.\n- Never trust client-side role information.\n- Validate attendance submissions on trusted backend infrastructure.\n- Prevent duplicate attendance records.\n- Prevent unauthorized attendance modifications.\n- Protect student and parent information.\n- Follow the principle of least privilege.\n\nDo not assume Firebase Spark Plan supports every backend capability. Identify paid-plan requirements, external service dependencies, and deployment requirements before integrating them.\n\n# 14. Implementation Rules\n\nFollow these instructions strictly:\n\n1. First inspect the complete existing project.\n2. Identify existing features and preserve working functionality.\n3. Remove Late Attendance completely.\n4. Prepare a clear implementation plan.\n5. Upgrade the existing application instead of building an unrelated new project.\n6. Reuse existing components and services wherever appropriate.\n7. Maintain existing database compatibility and migrate legacy data safely.\n8. Implement real functionality, not just UI mockups.\n9. Do not use fake data for production features.\n10. Do not leave buttons or forms non-functional.\n11. Add proper loading, success, and error states.\n12. Validate all user inputs.\n13. Handle edge cases and network failures.\n14. Test authentication, role permissions, attendance workflows, and security rules.\n15. Fix build errors and runtime errors.\n16. Update README documentation and deployment instructions.\n\n# 15. STRICT EXCLUSIONS\n\nDo NOT implement:\n- Face Recognition.\n- Facial Verification.\n- Biometric Authentication.\n- Face Enrollment.\n- Facial Embeddings.\n- Liveness Detection.\n- Biometric Data Storage.\n\nKeep the attendance process based on the existing QR, GPS, authentication, and authorized verification mechanisms.\n\n# FINAL OBJECTIVE\n\nTransform the existing project into a complete, secure, modern and scalable:\n\nSMART CAMPUS ATTENDANCE MANAGEMENT SYSTEM\n\nThe final application should feel like a professional campus management SaaS product rather than a basic college QR attendance project.\n\nPrioritize functionality, security, usability, database integrity, and maintainable architecture over unnecessary visual effects.\n\nDo not stop after generating a plan or redesigning the UI. Implement the features in the existing codebase, verify them, and provide a final summary of:\n\n- Completed features.\n- Modified files.\n- Database changes.\n- Security improvements.\n- Deployment requirements.\n- Firebase plan requirements.\n- Any features that still require external services.\n\nEnsure all three attendance statuses remain strictly PRESENT, ABSENT, and LEAVE."
    items {
      text: "# ROLE: Senior Software Architect, Full-Stack Developer & UI/UX Engineer\n\nAct as a Senior Software Architect and Full-Stack Developer. Your task is to upgrade my existing GitHub project into a professional, production-quality Smart Campus Attendance Management System.\n\n**Existing GitHub Repository:**\nhttps://github.com/pankajadhikari820/mini-attendence.git\n\nFirst, thoroughly inspect the entire existing codebase, including frontend, backend, Firebase configuration, Firestore collections, authentication, security rules, user roles, and existing attendance workflows.\n\nDo not blindly rewrite the project. Understand the existing architecture before making changes.\n\n# 1. CRITICAL: Remove Late Attendance Completely\n\nCompletely eliminate the Late Attendance feature from the entire project.\n\nThe system must support ONLY these three attendance statuses:\n\n- PRESENT\n- ABSENT\n- LEAVE\n\nRequirements:\n- Remove LATE status from all frontend components.\n- Remove late attendance calculations and conditions.\n- Remove late-related dashboard statistics.\n- Remove late-related reports, filters, labels, and notifications.\n- Update Firestore-related logic and validation wherever necessary.\n- Ensure no legacy code can generate or display a LATE status.\n\nDo not introduce any additional attendance status.\n\n# 2. Digital Student ID Card\n\nCreate a professional digital student identity card.\n\nFeatures:\n- Student name and photograph.\n- Unique student ID / enrollment number.\n- Department and course.\n- Semester and section.\n- College name and logo.\n- Unique QR code linked to the student\'s authenticated identity.\n- Downloadable digital ID card in PDF format.\n\nEnsure the ID QR code cannot independently authorize attendance.\n\n# 3. Advanced Attendance Analytics\n\nCreate a modern analytics dashboard with:\n\n- Weekly and monthly attendance graphs.\n- Overall attendance percentage.\n- Subject-wise attendance.\n- PRESENT, ABSENT, and LEAVE statistics.\n- Attendance trends.\n- Department-wise and class-wise analytics.\n- Date range filters.\n- Interactive charts and downloadable reports.\n\n# 4. Low Attendance Alert System\n\nImplement an automated attendance monitoring system.\n\nRequirements:\n- Set 75% as the default attendance threshold.\n- Automatically identify students whose attendance percentage falls below 75%.\n- Notify students and authorized faculty.\n- Display low-attendance warnings on dashboards.\n- Show subject-wise attendance shortage.\n- Avoid duplicate notifications.\n- Allow authorized administrators to configure the threshold.\n\n# 5. Parent Portal\n\nCreate a separate, secure parent dashboard.\n\nFeatures:\n- Parent login.\n- Link parent accounts with verified student accounts.\n- View child\'s attendance percentage.\n- View subject-wise attendance.\n- View attendance history.\n- View leave applications and approval status.\n- Download attendance reports.\n- Receive low-attendance alerts.\n\nSecurity:\n- Parents must only access their linked child\'s information.\n- Parent accounts must not have permission to modify attendance.\n- Implement proper account verification and access control.\n\n# 6. Subject-wise and Classroom Attendance\n\nUpgrade the existing attendance workflow.\n\nFeatures:\n- Department management.\n- Course management.\n- Semester management.\n- Section management.\n- Classroom management.\n- Subject management.\n- Faculty-to-subject assignment.\n- Faculty-to-class assignment.\n- Subject-specific attendance sessions.\n- Student enrollment in subjects.\n\nFaculty should only be able to create and manage attendance sessions for their assigned classes and subjects.\n\n# 7. Complete Audit Log System\n\nImplement immutable audit logs for important system actions.\n\nTrack:\n- Attendance creation.\n- Attendance modification.\n- Attendance correction.\n- Leave approval and rejection.\n- Student account changes.\n- Faculty actions.\n- Role and permission changes.\n- Administrative actions.\n\nEach audit record should include:\n- User ID.\n- User role.\n- Action performed.\n- Target record.\n- Timestamp.\n- Relevant metadata.\n\nPrevent normal users and administrators from editing or deleting audit history. Use trusted backend operations and appropriate security rules.\n\n# 8. Smart Timetable Integration\n\nBuild a timetable management system.\n\nFeatures:\n- Weekly timetable.\n- Subject scheduling.\n- Faculty scheduling.\n- Classroom allocation.\n- Automatic attendance session creation.\n- Session start and end times.\n- Conflict detection.\n- Automatic reminders for faculty.\n- Prevent duplicate sessions.\n\nAttendance sessions must follow the configured timetable and authorized faculty assignments.\n\n# 9. Student Performance Dashboard\n\nCreate a personalized student dashboard.\n\nDisplay:\n- Overall attendance percentage.\n- Subject-wise attendance.\n- Weekly attendance trends.\n- Monthly progress.\n- Attendance shortage warnings.\n- Recent attendance history.\n- Leave history.\n- Upcoming timetable.\n\nUse clean charts, statistics, and modern visual components.\n\n# 10. Professional Excel/PDF Reporting\n\nImplement report generation for:\n\n- Individual students.\n- Individual subjects.\n- Classes and sections.\n- Departments.\n- Monthly attendance.\n- Date-range attendance.\n- PRESENT, ABSENT, and LEAVE summaries.\n\nSupport:\n- Excel export.\n- Professional PDF export.\n- College branding.\n- Generation timestamps.\n- Proper report formatting.\n\n# 11. Role-Based Access Control\n\nMaintain and strengthen the existing role hierarchy.\n\nSupport:\n- Super Admin.\n- Organization Admin.\n- Manager / Faculty.\n- Student.\n- Parent.\n\nEnforce permissions at the backend and Firestore Security Rules level.\n\nDo not rely only on hiding frontend buttons.\n\n# 12. UI/UX Modernization\n\nUpgrade the complete application interface to a premium SaaS-level design.\n\nDesign direction:\n- Modern professional dashboard.\n- Clean typography.\n- Consistent spacing.\n- Responsive layouts.\n- Professional sidebar navigation.\n- Attractive analytics cards.\n- Interactive graphs.\n- Smooth but subtle animations.\n- Consistent design system.\n- Light and dark modes.\n- Mobile-friendly student and parent dashboards.\n\nAvoid excessive gradients, unnecessary animations, and cluttered layouts.\n\nMaintain the existing college identity and branding wherever available.\n\n# 13. Firebase Architecture and Security\n\nPreserve the existing Firebase architecture wherever practical.\n\nReview and improve:\n- Firebase Authentication.\n- Cloud Firestore.\n- Firestore Security Rules.\n- Firebase Storage.\n- Cloud Functions, if available and required.\n- Data validation.\n- Role-based permissions.\n- Database indexes.\n- Secure API operations.\n\nImportant:\n- Never expose Firebase Admin credentials or private API keys.\n- Never trust client-side role information.\n- Validate attendance submissions on trusted backend infrastructure.\n- Prevent duplicate attendance records.\n- Prevent unauthorized attendance modifications.\n- Protect student and parent information.\n- Follow the principle of least privilege.\n\nDo not assume Firebase Spark Plan supports every backend capability. Identify paid-plan requirements, external service dependencies, and deployment requirements before integrating them.\n\n# 14. Implementation Rules\n\nFollow these instructions strictly:\n\n1. First inspect the complete existing project.\n2. Identify existing features and preserve working functionality.\n3. Remove Late Attendance completely.\n4. Prepare a clear implementation plan.\n5. Upgrade the existing application instead of building an unrelated new project.\n6. Reuse existing components and services wherever appropriate.\n7. Maintain existing database compatibility and migrate legacy data safely.\n8. Implement real functionality, not just UI mockups.\n9. Do not use fake data for production features.\n10. Do not leave buttons or forms non-functional.\n11. Add proper loading, success, and error states.\n12. Validate all user inputs.\n13. Handle edge cases and network failures.\n14. Test authentication, role permissions, attendance workflows, and security rules.\n15. Fix build errors and runtime errors.\n16. Update README documentation and deployment instructions.\n\n# 15. STRICT EXCLUSIONS\n\nDo NOT implement:\n- Face Recognition.\n- Facial Verification.\n- Biometric Authentication.\n- Face Enrollment.\n- Facial Embeddings.\n- Liveness Detection.\n- Biometric Data Storage.\n\nKeep the attendance process based on the existing QR, GPS, authentication, and authorized verification mechanisms.\n\n# FINAL OBJECTIVE\n\nTransform the existing project into a complete, secure, modern and scalable:\n\nSMART CAMPUS ATTENDANCE MANAGEMENT SYSTEM\n\nThe final application should feel like a professional campus management SaaS product rather than a basic college QR attendance project.\n\nPrioritize functionality, security, usability, database integrity, and maintainable architecture over unnecessary visual effects.\n\nDo not stop after generating a plan or redesigning the UI. Implement the features in the existing codebase, verify them, and provide a final summary of:\n\n- Completed features.\n- Modified files.\n- Database changes.\n- Security improvements.\n- Deployment requirements.\n- Firebase plan requirements.\n- Any features that still require external services.\n\nEnsure all three attendance statuses remain strictly PRESENT, ABSENT, and LEAVE."
    }
  }
}
