  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Uncovering Root Causes**\n\nI\'ve just finished a comprehensive code audit and the results are incredibly insightful. I\'ve compiled a preliminary list of *every* root cause identified for both the Live Session issues and the multi-teacher/subject-wise attendance problems. The clarity is astonishing! I feel as though, with this, we are one step closer to solving the issues.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Specific Errors**\n\nI\'ve isolated the critical issue within the \"No active session right now\" error. It boils down to a single-parent lock-in, where the query only considered the manager ID, excluding sessions started by other teachers. Plus, a missing composite index caused failures to be silently swallowed.\n\n\n**Analyzing Security Rules**\n\nI\'ve been digging into the Firestore security rules and it\'s yielding crucial insights. It\'s clear that the rules statically evaluate queries against `allow read` / `allow list`. I see an opportunity here to improve the visibility of sessions by examining those rules in greater detail. Specifically, I\'m focusing on lines 166-170 of the `firestore.rules` file; this looks to be the key to better visibility control and addressing permission-denied states.\n\n\n**Discovering Core Issues**\n\nI\'ve just had a breakthrough! Analyzing `isDirectChildOfSession` in the security rules exposed a major root cause. The Firestore rules are rejecting queries based on missing conditions within the `where` clauses. Specifically, the rules require `sess.ownerRole == \'manager\'` to be guaranteed in a student\'s query, which isn\'t always included. This oversight, combined with `subscribeActiveParentSession`\'s handling of errors, is leading to permission-denied states and silent failures, which I did not see before this analysis.\n\n\n**Confirming Critical Mismatch**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed a major Firestore Rules query-constraint mismatch bug! Every student is getting \"permission-denied\" errors. My analysis shows the rules require `ownerRole == \'manager\'` for read access, but student queries don\'t always guarantee this. Similar issues affect manager and admin queries. This gap is causing widespread permission-denied states and silent failures due to an oversight.\n\n\n**Identifying Authorization Patterns**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the authorization patterns in detail. I\'ve realized that if a student query includes `User().role == \'member\'`, along with matching `orgId`, `classId`, and `sectionId` (or `ownerUid`), Firestore statically proves authorization. However, I need to ensure `ownerRole == \'manager\'` is also included. I\'m focusing on ensuring these conditions are consistently implemented in queries to prevent further access issues. I\'m now certain this is key to getting deterministic authorization working and ending permission-denied errors.\n\n\n**Validating Data Consistency**\n\nI\'ve been thinking about the implications of the current attendance data structure. I\'m satisfied now that using `${orgId}_${sessionId}_${studentUid}` is clean, elegant and avoids conflicts, even with overlapping sessions. This ensures no data overwrites and supports legacy daily reports. This also facilitates simultaneous teacher-led sessions without data issues.\n\n\n**Eliminating Late Attendance**\n\nI\'ve decided to remove the \'late\' status from attendance. I\'ll change `AttendanceStatus` to \'present\', \'absent\', or \'leave\' only. I\'ll convert all legacy \'late\' statuses to \'present\' on read, ensure the security rules only allow the new statuses, and eliminate all traces of \'late\' from the settings and helper files. I\'m focusing on ensuring data consistency and simplifying the system.\n\n\n**Implementing New Features**\n\nI\'m now focused on incorporating the new requirements into the project. I\'m adding `xlsx` for Excel exports, which will create formatted workbooks with multiple sheets for comprehensive reporting. Also, I\'m extending the `UserRole` enum to include \'super\\_admin\', \'parent\', and keeping backward compatibility for existing user roles, ensuring the system remains fully functional.\n\n\n**Defining User Roles**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the new user roles and their associated permissions, mapping the system access levels: Super Admin, Organization Admin, Manager/Faculty/Teacher, and Student. I\'m ensuring Super Admins can manage the entire system, Organization Admins manage their org, Managers have teacher dashboards, and Students see their personalized data. I will make a table for my next steps and share them for review.\n\n\n**Designing New Features**\n\nI\'m now integrating the new features into the system design, specifically focusing on the parent portal and comprehensive attendance reporting. I\'m prioritizing real-time updates via `onSnapshot` for zero-refresh data and enabling direct QR code scanning/entry for attendance. I also plan to add subject-wise and overall attendance percentages, trends, warnings, and histories. The parent portal will offer read-only access to linked children\'s attendance data and reporting, secured by strict Firestore rules. I need to make sure the data structure supports everything.\n\n\n**Refining Data Structures**\n\nI\'m now refining the data structures to optimally represent courses, subjects, faculty assignments, and timetables. The `{classId}` structure looks solid. I am also working on `parentLinks` to include student-parent relationships. I\'m focusing on ensuring data integrity and efficient queries, and the relationships are essential to the new designs.\n\n\n**Optimizing Field Structures**\n\nI\'ve been working to enhance the database field structures to improve functionality. I\'ve been optimizing the `UserProfile`, `AttendanceSession`, and `AttendanceRecord` fields to increase compatibility, adding various optional fields. My goal is to ensure data consistency and enable efficient queries.\n\n\n**Refining Permission Checks**\n\nI\'m now implementing the final changes in `firestore.rules`, focusing on active sessions and user organization scope. The key is strict access control; I\'m making sure queries are scoped to active sessions within the user\'s organization. Specifically, I\'m verifying the `orgId` and ensuring only the correct org data is accessible.\n\n\n**Refining Rule Logic**\n\nI\'m now revising the Firestore rules again to simplify the read authorization logic. I\'m focusing on ensuring users can read session details within their organization, regardless of their role. I\'ll maintain that the rule structure must be clean, query compatible, and exclude false rejections, regardless of role.\n\n\n**Verifying Rule Compatibility**\n\nI\'m now deeply involved in verifying static query compatibility within the Firestore rules. Specifically, I\'m examining the scenario where a student query uses `where(\'orgId\', \'==\', myOrgId)` and `where(\'classId\', \'==\', myClassId)`. I\'m focused on guaranteeing that these conditions are sufficient to satisfy the security rules for read access to session data, and the role of the user. I need to make sure that the rules are allowing access within the appropriate constraints.\n\n\n**Confirming Static Provenance**\n\nI\'ve just been deeply validating various query scenarios with the Firestore Rules engine to verify authorization logic. I\'ve confirmed that student and manager queries using appropriate conditions (orgId, ownerUid, etc.) are 100% statically provable by the engine. I\'ve also found an elegant optimization for the manager query using `sess.ownerRole == \'admin\'` and a specific `where` clause to eliminate extra `get()` reads, improving efficiency and ensuring static provability. Plus, I\'ll allow `canSelfMarkAttendance` in `firestore.rules` to accept both.\n\n\n\n**Validating Session Compatibility**\n\nI\'m now carefully checking the compatibility of different session types with the authorization rules, specifically for class-based student sessions, manager-based legacy sessions, and admin-to-manager sessions. I\'ve reconfirmed the requirement for unique `recordId`s based on `orgId`, `sessionId`, and `studentUid`. My focus is on ensuring a secure and interference-free environment for simultaneous or back-to-back sessions.\n\n\n**Verifying Attendance Security**\n\nI\'m now deeply focused on ensuring stringent security for student attendance submissions. I\'m carefully analyzing the conditions to reject duplicate submissions (`!exists(attendance/{orgId}_{S1}_{studentUid})`) and ensure that QR codes older than 10 seconds, or for closed/expired sessions, are also rejected in the `firestore.rules`.\n\n\n**Expanding System Functionality**\n\nI\'m now integrating new features into the system. I\'m focusing on one-click PDF ID card downloads, using jsPDF to generate branded documents with embedded QR codes. I\'m also developing the `/academics` page, the timetable manager, with conflict detection. Plus, audit logs are being incorporated.\n\n\n**Implementing Audit Logs**\n\nI\'m now implementing comprehensive audit logs for the system. I\'m tracking every significant action, including attendance updates, session changes, and user management events. All actions are immutable within the Firestore rules and are viewable and filterable by Admins and Super Admins, with support for CSV/PDF export.\n\n\n**Analyzing Report Functions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on implementing the new reporting features. I\'m ensuring the analytics overview includes overall percentages, subject-wise breakdowns via bar charts, and trends for both weekly and monthly attendance, with alerts for students below a threshold. I\'m focusing on the filters in the reports page, and am readying the features for professional PDF and Excel exports.\n\n\n\n"
