      text: "Build a complete, fully working, fully responsive web app called \"Smart Attendance System\".\nIt is a multi-organization, QR-based attendance platform with a hierarchy:\nOrganization (top) \342\206\222 Managers \342\206\222 Members.\nExample: College \342\206\222 Teachers \342\206\222 Students. Company \342\206\222 Team Leads \342\206\222 Employees.\nEverything must really work with the real Firebase project below. No mock data in the real app, no TODOs, no fake buttons.\n\n==================================================\n1. FIREBASE (USE EXACTLY THIS CONFIG IN src/firebase.ts)\n==================================================\nUse Firebase Authentication (Email/Password) and Cloud Firestore.\n\nconst firebaseConfig = {\n  apiKey: \"AIzaSyB4O0_f2y6D3No4kKw3PALtfO_6v2s6lAQ\",\n  authDomain: \"attendence-92251.firebaseapp.com\",\n  projectId: \"attendence-92251\",\n  storageBucket: \"attendence-92251.firebasestorage.app\",\n  messagingSenderId: \"327288246371\",\n  appId: \"1:327288246371:web:bf97d084314aa9ea726856\",\n  measurementId: \"G-FQP60QRQRP\"\n};\n\nExport `auth` and `db` from `src/firebase.ts`. Use the modular Firebase v9+ SDK.\n\nIMPORTANT TECHNICAL RULE: when an Admin or Manager creates a login account for someone else, the current user must NOT be signed out. Use a SECONDARY Firebase app instance (`initializeApp(config, \"secondary\")`) to create the new user with `createUserWithEmailAndPassword`, then sign out of the secondary instance only. After creating, also send a password-reset email so the person can set their own password.\n\n==================================================\n2. DESIGN: SIMPLE, CALM, EDITORIAL\n==================================================\nUse this page ONLY as a visual reference: https://saas-landing-page-template-for-claude-code-free-ht-v1.21st.app/\nCopy its FEEL, not its content or brand:\n- Lots of white space, minimal, no clutter.\n- One big bold headline per section with one short line below it.\n- Plain, simple sentences. No jargon. Buttons are 1 to 3 words (\"Scan QR\", \"Add member\").\n- Numbered steps for \"How it works\". Simple rounded cards, thin borders, soft shadows.\n- One warm accent color on a soft off-white background, plus a clean dark mode toggle.\n- Sticky top bar: logo, few links, one main button (\"Start free\"), hamburger menu on mobile.\n- FAQ as accordions. Small contact form at the bottom. Clean footer.\n- Subtle animations only (fade/slide on scroll).\n- Fonts: \"Inter\" for English, \"Noto Sans Devanagari\" for Hindi.\n\n==================================================\n3. LANGUAGE SWITCH: ENGLISH / \340\244\271\340\244\277\340\244\202\340\244\246\340\245\200\n==================================================\n- Toggle \"EN | \340\244\271\340\244\277\340\244\202\" in the landing page top bar AND inside the app header.\n- The whole UI switches instantly without reload: landing, forms, dashboards, buttons, errors, toasts, empty states, reports, dates and numbers.\n- Use a simple i18n setup: `en.json` + `hi.json` with identical keys, a `useTranslation` hook, and a LanguageContext.\n- Save the choice in localStorage and in the user\'s Firestore profile. Default is English.\n- User-entered data (names, notes, org name) is never translated.\n- Hindi must be natural and simple, not word-for-word. Examples: Mark Attendance = \"\340\244\271\340\244\276\340\244\234\340\244\274\340\244\277\340\244\260\340\245\200 \340\244\262\340\244\227\340\244\276\340\244\217\340\244\201\", Present = \"\340\244\211\340\244\252\340\244\270\340\245\215\340\244\245\340\244\277\340\244\244\", Absent = \"\340\244\205\340\244\250\340\245\201\340\244\252\340\244\270\340\245\215\340\244\245\340\244\277\340\244\244\", Late = \"\340\244\246\340\245\207\340\244\260 \340\244\270\340\245\207\", Leave = \"\340\244\233\340\245\201\340\244\237\340\245\215\340\244\237\340\245\200\".\n\n==================================================\n4. LANDING PAGE\n==================================================\n1. Hero: headline \"Attendance in one scan.\", one line, buttons \"Start free\" and \"See how it works\", and a mock QR card showing \"Present \342\234\223\".\n2. How it works (3 numbered steps): Create your organization \342\206\222 Add your people \342\206\222 Scan QR to mark attendance.\n3. What you get (6 items): QR attendance, Manual attendance, Reports, Leave requests, Works on phone and laptop, Hindi and English.\n4. Who is it for: College, School, Coaching, Company (4 cards, each showing its hierarchy).\n5. \"Simple. Fast. Safe.\" (three short reasons)\n6. FAQ (5 questions): Is it free? Do members need an app? How is the QR made? Can one person see another\'s data? Does it work on mobile?\n7. Contact form (name, email, message) saved to Firestore `messages`.\n8. Footer with links and the language toggle.\n\n==================================================\n5. TECH STACK\n==================================================\nReact + Vite + TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, React Router, `qrcode.react` (generate QR), `html5-qrcode` (camera scan), `recharts` (charts), `lucide-react` (icons), `framer-motion` (light animation), `papaparse` (CSV import/export), `jspdf` + `jspdf-autotable` (PDF export).\n\n==================================================\n6. ROLES AND PERMISSIONS\n==================================================\nRole names are customizable per organization (default Admin / Manager / Member; the org can rename Manager \342\206\222 \"Teacher\", Member \342\206\222 \"Student\"). The whole UI uses these labels in both languages.\n\n1. ORG ADMIN: creates the organization; adds, edits, deactivates, removes Managers; approves or rejects pending registrations; marks attendance of Managers; sees all Managers, Members and reports of the org; approves Manager leave requests; posts announcements; edits settings.\n2. MANAGER: adds Members (or approves those who join with the Manager Code); marks attendance of THEIR OWN Members only; sees reports of own Members only; approves Member leave requests; posts announcements to own Members; has own QR and ID so the Admin can mark them.\n3. MEMBER: sees own QR and Member ID (download image / print ID card); sees own history, monthly % and calendar; sends leave requests; cannot see anyone else\'s data.\n\nStrict isolation: no user can read or write another organization\'s data. A Manager can never see another Manager\'s Members.\n\n==================================================\n7. REGISTRATION AND ONBOARDING\n==================================================\n- Create organization: Admin signs up with email, password, org name, org type (College / School / Coaching / Company / Other). A unique 6-character Organization Code is generated (check it is not already used).\n- Direct add: Admin adds a Manager, Manager adds a Member (name, email, phone, optional roll/employee number). A readable unique ID is generated with an atomic counter in a Firestore transaction (e.g. `ORG-MGR-0001`, `ORG-MEM-0001`), and a QR is created.\n- Bulk import: Admin/Manager uploads a CSV (name, email, phone) to add many people at once, with a preview and error report before saving.\n- Self-registration: a person picks a role and enters the Organization Code (Managers) or the Manager Code (Members). Status stays \"pending\" until the parent approves. Pending users see a friendly \"Waiting for approval\" screen.\n- QR content is JSON: `{ \"orgId\", \"uid\", \"memberId\" }`.\n- \"Download QR\" and \"Print ID Card\" (name, ID, QR, org name).\n\n==================================================\n8. ATTENDANCE FLOW\n==================================================\n- \"Mark Attendance\" is the main button after login.\n- Mark Attendance page (Admin and Manager) has two tabs:\n  1. SCAN QR: opens the camera, scans, validates, marks Present. Shows a big success or error card with name, initials avatar and time. The scanner stays open for continuous scanning. Include beep + vibration on success, a \"Scanned today: N\" counter, a front/back camera switch, and a 2-second pause on the same QR to avoid double scans.\n  2. MANUAL: type ID or search by name, choose Present / Absent / Late / Leave, optional note. Also \"Mark all present\", then untick exceptions with checkboxes.\n- Rules:\n  - Only the direct parent can mark (Admin \342\206\222 Managers, Manager \342\206\222 own Members).\n  - One record per person per date. Deterministic document ID `{orgId}_{date}_{personUid}`. If already marked, show \"Already marked today\".\n  - Pending and inactive users cannot be marked.\n  - The parent can correct a record on the same day, and Admin can correct any day.\n  - Org setting \"Late after\" time (e.g. 09:30): a QR scan after that time is saved as \"Late\" automatically.\n  - Use the server timestamp for the time, and a date in the org\'s local time zone (default Asia/Kolkata).\n- Optional anti-cheating (toggle in settings, off by default): \"Location check\". The marker\'s device must be within N meters of the organization\'s saved location. Save the location with one tap (\"Set my current location\").\n- Record fields: orgId, personUid, personName, role, managerId, date (YYYY-MM-DD), timestamp, status, method (\"qr\" | \"manual\"), markedBy, note.\n\n==================================================\n9. LEAVE REQUESTS, ANNOUNCEMENTS, NOTIFICATIONS\n==================================================\n- Member sends a leave request (from, to, reason) to their Manager. Manager sends to Admin.\n- The parent sees pending requests and taps Approve or Reject (with optional comment). Approved days automatically show as \"Leave\" in reports and calendar.\n- Admin and Manager can post short announcements. They show on the dashboards of everyone below.\n- In-app notification bell with unread count (new approval, leave decision, announcement). No external services needed.\n\n==================================================\n10. DASHBOARDS AND REPORTS\n==================================================\n- Admin: total Managers, total Members, today\'s Present / Absent / Late, 7-day and 30-day chart, Managers list with today\'s status, pending approvals.\n- Manager: my Members, today\'s numbers, \"Low attendance\" list (below 75%, threshold configurable), weekly chart, pending leave requests.\n- Member: my QR, today\'s status, this month\'s %, a calendar with colors (green Present, red Absent, yellow Late/Leave), current streak.\n- Reports page: filters (date range, person, status), table, chart, summary per person (present days, absent days, late days, %), and a \"monthly report\" view like a register sheet. Export CSV and PDF. Search, sort and pagination on all lists.\n- Big numbers and simple labels, in the same calm style as the landing page.\n\n==================================================\n11. FIRESTORE DATA MODEL\n==================================================\n- `organizations/{orgId}`: name, type, orgCode, adminUid, roleLabels {manager, member}, lateAfter, timezone, location {lat, lng, radius, enabled}, lowAttendanceThreshold, counters {manager, member}, createdAt\n- `users/{uid}`: uid, name, email, phone, role, orgId, managerId, memberId, status (\"pending\" | \"active\" | \"inactive\"), managerCode, language, createdAt\n- `attendance/{orgId_date_personUid}`: fields from section 8\n- `leaves/{id}`: orgId, personUid, toUid, fromDate, toDate, reason, status, comment, createdAt\n- `announcements/{id}`: orgId, fromUid, audience, text, createdAt\n- `notifications/{id}`: orgId, toUid, type, text, read, createdAt\n- `messages/{id}`: contact form entries (create-only for public)\n- A lookup collection `orgCodes/{orgCode}` \342\206\222 { orgId } and `managerCodes/{code}` \342\206\222 { orgId, managerUid } so that self-registration can find the parent WITHOUT reading other users\' data.\nList all required composite indexes clearly.\n\n==================================================\n12. SECURITY AND AUTH\n==================================================\n- Email/Password sign-in, \"Forgot password\", logout, persistent session, route guards by role, redirect to the correct dashboard after login.\n- Write complete `firestore.rules` as a separate file:\n  - Users read only their own org\'s data.\n  - Admin has full control inside their own org.\n  - Manager reads and writes only attendance of their own Members.\n  - Member reads only their own records.\n  - Users cannot change their own `role`, `orgId`, `status`, `managerId` or `memberId`.\n  - Attendance can be created only by the person\'s direct parent, and only for active people.\n  - Public may only create `messages`, and read the `orgCodes` / `managerCodes` lookups by exact document ID (no listing).\n- README must contain the Firebase steps: enable Email/Password, create Firestore (asia-south1), publish rules, add authorized domains, create indexes.\n\n==================================================\n13. RESPONSIVE AND UX\n==================================================\n- Mobile-first. Phone: bottom navigation. Tablet and laptop: sidebar. Works from 360px to large desktop.\n- Big touch-friendly buttons on the scanner page.\n- Loading skeletons, friendly empty states with one clear button, simple validation errors, toast notifications.\n- Clear camera-permission message (camera needs HTTPS).\n- Installable PWA (manifest + icons).\n- Accessible: labels, contrast, keyboard friendly.\n- Handle errors like \"email already in use\", \"wrong password\", \"no internet\" with clear, translated messages.\n\n==================================================\n14. PAGES\n==================================================\nLanding, Login, Register (Create Org / Join Org), Forgot Password, Waiting for Approval, Admin Dashboard, Manage Managers, Manager Dashboard, Manage Members, Mark Attendance, Leave Requests, Announcements, Reports, Member Dashboard (QR + history), Profile and Settings (org name, role labels, late time, location check, language), 404.\n\n==================================================\n15. DELIVERABLES\n==================================================\n- Clean folder structure: components, pages, hooks, services, context, i18n, types.\n- `src/firebase.ts` (with the config above), `firestore.rules`, `en.json`, `hi.json`, README with setup and run steps.\n- A \"Load demo data\" button in Admin settings (1 org, 3 Managers, 15 Members, 30 days of realistic attendance) so I can demo easily. Demo data must be clearly marked and removable with a \"Delete demo data\" button."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "UPDATE the existing \"Smart Attendance System\" app. Keep everything that already works (roles, hierarchy, dashboards, reports, leave requests, Hindi/English toggle, Firebase config, PWA). Make ONLY the changes below, and update `firestore.rules`, indexes, `en.json`, `hi.json` and README accordingly. Do not leave anything half-done.\n\n==================================================\n1. HOME PAGE CHANGES\n==================================================\n- Replace every \"Start free\" / \"Start free trial\" button (top bar, hero, any other section) with \"Sign up\" (Hindi: \"\340\244\270\340\244\276\340\244\207\340\244\250 \340\244\205\340\244\252 \340\244\225\340\244\260\340\245\207\340\244\202\"). Keep a \"Login\" button too.\n- In the hero section, remove the mock QR code card. Put a clean \"Mark your attendance\" card there instead (Hindi: \"\340\244\205\340\244\252\340\244\250\340\245\200 \340\244\271\340\244\276\340\244\234\340\244\274\340\244\277\340\244\260\340\245\200 \340\244\262\340\244\227\340\244\276\340\244\217\340\244\201\") with one short line and two buttons: \"Scan QR\" and \"Enter ID\". Both open /mark. If the user is not logged in, send them to Login first, then return to /mark.\n- Keep the same calm, minimal style as before.\n\n==================================================\n2. NEW 6-CHARACTER UNIQUE ID (replaces the ORG-MEM-0001 style)\n==================================================\n- Format: 3 letters + 3 digits. Examples: PAN482, RAH071.\n- Letters: first 3 English letters of the person\'s name (uppercase, ignore spaces and symbols; if shorter than 3 letters, pad with X; if the name is typed in Hindi, ask for the English spelling for the ID).\n- Digits: random 3 digits 000 to 999.\n- Must be unique across the whole app. Use a Firestore transaction with a lookup collection `userIds/{ID}` \342\206\222 { uid, orgId, role }. If the ID already exists, generate new digits (retry up to 30 times, then change the third letter).\n- Same format for Admin, Managers and Members. Show it everywhere: profile, ID card, lists, reports, search.\n- The personal QR on the ID card now encodes { orgId, uid, id }.\n\n==================================================\n3. LIVE ROTATING QR SESSION (main way to mark attendance)\n==================================================\nWho starts it: Admin starts a session for Managers. A Manager starts a session for their own Members.\n\na) \"Start attendance\" page asks: session title (optional), duration (15 / 30 / 60 / 120 min), allowed radius (default 50 m, range 20 to 500 m), and sets the LOCATION: take the starter\'s current GPS (high accuracy) and show it on a small map (Leaflet + OpenStreetMap, no API key needed) with a circle for the radius. The starter can drag the pin to adjust it.\nb) The session screen (\"projector mode\", works full screen) shows a big QR that CHANGES EVERY 10 SECONDS with a countdown ring, and below it a 6-digit live code that changes at the same time. Also show a live counter \"Marked: 23 / 40\", a real-time list of names as they mark, and \"Close session\" and \"Extend 10 min\" buttons. Keep the screen awake using the Screen Wake Lock API.\nc) QR content: { \"s\": sessionId, \"t\": token }. The token is a random 128-bit string from crypto.getRandomValues. The live code is a random 6-digit number.\nd) Every 10 seconds the starter\'s device writes a new token and code to `sessionSecrets/{sessionId}` (keep `prevToken`, `prevCode`, and `tokenUpdatedAt = serverTimestamp()`).\ne) A token is valid for max 15 seconds after it is created. The previous token stays valid for 5 more seconds after rotation (network delay). Screenshots or forwarded QR codes become useless within seconds.\n\n==================================================\n4. MEMBER SIDE: \"MARK YOUR ATTENDANCE\" PAGE (/mark)\n==================================================\n- Step 1 (automatic): ask for location permission, read GPS, and show \"You are 12 m from the class \342\234\223\" or \"You are 340 m away \342\234\227\". If GPS accuracy is worse than 100 m, ask the user to try again.\n- Step 2: two tabs:\n  1. Scan QR: camera scanner for the live QR.\n  2. Enter ID: fields \"Your ID\" (6 characters, must match the logged-in user\'s own ID) and \"Live code\" (the 6 digits shown on the screen).\n- Success: big green \"Attendance marked\" with name, time and distance.\n- Clear, translated errors: \"QR expired, scan again\", \"You are outside the allowed area\", \"Session closed\", \"Already marked today\", \"This device was already used by someone else in this session\", \"No active session right now\".\n- Find the active session automatically: the open session of the user\'s own parent (Member \342\206\222 their Manager, Manager \342\206\222 Admin).\n- If marked after the organization\'s \"Late after\" time, the status is \"late\".\n\n==================================================\n5. SECURITY (enforce in Firestore rules, not only in the UI; do NOT use Cloud Functions, we are on the free plan)\n==================================================\n- Split session data into two documents:\n  - `sessions/{sessionId}`: orgId, ownerUid, ownerRole, title, status (\"open\" | \"closed\"), startedAt, endsAt, location { lat, lng, cosLat, radius }, markedCount. Readable by the owner and the owner\'s direct children.\n  - `sessionSecrets/{sessionId}`: token, prevToken, code, prevCode, tokenUpdatedAt. Readable and writable ONLY by the owner. Members must NEVER be able to read it (otherwise they could mark attendance from home).\n- Attendance create rule for self-marking (method \"live_qr\" or \"live_code\"): the user is logged in, active, and a direct child of the session owner; the session is open and not past `endsAt`; the token or code matches `sessionSecrets` (current one within 15 s of `tokenUpdatedAt`, previous one within 5 s after rotation); `personUid == request.auth.uid`; `timestamp == request.time`; the document ID is `{orgId}_{date}_{personUid}` so no duplicates; and the reported lat/lng is inside the session radius, computed in the rules with a flat-earth approximation using the stored `cosLat` (math.sqrt and math.pow are available in Firestore rules).\n- Save in the attendance record: sessionId, method, lat, lng, accuracy, distanceMeters, deviceId.\n- One device = one person per session: create `sessionDevices/{sessionId_deviceId}` in the same batch as the attendance record, and make the rules reject it if it already exists (deviceId is a random id kept in localStorage).\n- Log failed attempts (outside radius, wrong or expired token) to `attempts/{id}` {sessionId, personUid, reason, distance, time}. The session owner sees a \"Rejected attempts\" list on the session screen.\n- Manual marking by Admin/Manager (typing the person\'s ID, or scanning the person\'s ID card QR) stays only as a BACKUP for cases like a dead phone or no GPS. Those records are tagged \"manual\" and shown in yellow in reports.\n- Organization settings: default radius, default session duration, and an on/off switch for \"Location check\" (default ON).\n\n==================================================\n6. FIRESTORE AND DELIVERABLES\n==================================================\n- New collections: `userIds`, `sessions`, `sessionSecrets`, `sessionDevices`, `attempts`. List all new composite indexes.\n- Give me the COMPLETE updated `firestore.rules` file.\n- Add all new text to en.json and hi.json in natural, simple Hindi (examples: \"Start attendance\" = \"\340\244\271\340\244\276\340\244\234\340\244\274\340\244\277\340\244\260\340\245\200 \340\244\266\340\245\201\340\244\260\340\245\202 \340\244\225\340\244\260\340\245\207\340\244\202\", \"Close session\" = \"\340\244\270\340\245\207\340\244\266\340\244\250 \340\244\254\340\244\202\340\244\246 \340\244\225\340\244\260\340\245\207\340\244\202\", \"You are outside the allowed area\" = \"\340\244\206\340\244\252 \340\244\244\340\244\257 \340\244\234\340\244\227\340\244\271 \340\244\270\340\245\207 \340\244\254\340\244\276\340\244\271\340\244\260 \340\244\271\340\245\210\340\244\202\").\n- Update the \"Load demo data\" button so demo users get IDs in the new format.\n- Everything must really work on mobile and laptop. No TODOs, no fake buttons."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "Implement a complete **Dynamic QR-Based Live Attendance System** in my existing Mini Attendance project.\n\nIMPORTANT: First analyze the entire existing project structure, authentication system, Firebase configuration, Firestore database structure, user roles, permissions, and existing Live Session functionality. Then implement the feature without breaking or removing any existing functionality.\n\n### 1. Higher Authority \342\200\223 Dynamic QR Generator\n\nThe Admin/Higher Authority who has permission to create a Live Attendance Session should be able to:\n\n- Start a Live Attendance Session.\n- Generate and display a QR code on the Live Session dashboard.\n- Automatically refresh/change the QR code every 10 seconds.\n- Display a visible 10-second countdown timer.\n- Generate a new, unique, secure QR token every 10 seconds.\n- Show the session status as LIVE.\n- Allow the authority to stop/end the live session.\n- Stop QR token generation immediately when the session ends.\n\n### 2. Lower Authority \342\200\223 QR Scanner\n\nUsers with the appropriate lower-level authority (such as Manager/Teacher) should have a dedicated QR Scanner option in their dashboard.\n\nThe scanner should:\n\n- Access the device camera with user permission.\n- Scan QR codes generated by the currently active Live Session.\n- Support mobile and desktop cameras.\n- Automatically detect and validate the QR code.\n- Mark attendance immediately after successful verification.\n- Display a success message after attendance is recorded.\n- Display clear error messages for expired, invalid, already-used, or inactive-session QR codes.\n\n### 3. Real-Time QR Validation\n\nThis is a critical requirement.\n\n- Each QR code must be valid for only 10 seconds.\n- After 10 seconds, the previous QR token must become invalid.\n- Only the currently active QR token should be accepted.\n- QR tokens must be generated and validated securely.\n- Do not rely only on frontend JavaScript timers for security.\n- Use Firebase Cloud Functions or another secure server-side validation mechanism if available.\n- Validate the token against the active session and its expiration time on the backend.\n- Prevent users from manually creating fake QR codes or modifying QR payloads.\n\n### 4. Attendance Recording in Firebase\n\nAfter a valid QR scan:\n\n- Identify the authenticated user.\n- Verify that the user is authorized to mark attendance.\n- Record attendance against the correct live session.\n- Save the user\'s UID, session ID, organization ID, timestamp, and attendance status.\n- Prevent duplicate attendance for the same user in the same session.\n- Use Firestore transactions or another atomic approach to prevent race conditions.\n\n### 5. Security and Permissions\n\nStrictly follow my existing role-based access control.\n\n- Only authorized Admin/Higher Authority users can create or stop Live Sessions.\n- Only authorized lower-level users can scan QR codes and mark attendance.\n- Students or unauthorized users must not be able to create sessions.\n- Validate organization ID and user permissions on the server.\n- Never expose Firebase Admin credentials or secret keys in frontend code.\n- Update Firestore Security Rules wherever required.\n\n### 6. UI/UX Requirements\n\nMaintain the existing project\'s design language and dashboard layout.\n\nFor the Live Session screen:\n- Large, clearly visible QR code.\n- 10-second countdown timer.\n- LIVE status indicator.\n- Session details.\n- Stop Session button.\n\nFor the Scanner screen:\n- Clean camera scanning interface.\n- Scanning frame.\n- Camera permission handling.\n- Success and error notifications.\n- Mobile-responsive design.\n\n### 7. Important Technical Requirements\n\n- Use my existing Firebase project and configuration.\n- Reuse existing authentication and user role systems.\n- Do not create unnecessary duplicate collections.\n- Maintain compatibility with the current database structure.\n- Handle network errors and expired tokens properly.\n- Ensure QR refresh works in real time.\n- Ensure attendance is recorded only once per user per session.\n\n### 8. Testing and Final Verification\n\nAfter implementation, test these scenarios:\n\n1. Admin starts a Live Session.\n2. QR code changes automatically every 10 seconds.\n3. An authorized Teacher/Manager scans the current QR.\n4. Attendance is successfully recorded.\n5. The previous QR code is rejected after expiration.\n6. Duplicate attendance is prevented.\n7. An unauthorized user cannot mark attendance.\n8. An ended session cannot accept new attendance.\n9. Multiple users can scan the same currently active QR code.\n10. The system works on both mobile and desktop.\n\n**FINAL INSTRUCTION:** Implement this feature directly in my existing project. Do not just provide sample code or explanations. Inspect the existing code, make the necessary changes, update Firebase rules and backend functions if required, and report all files modified and any Firebase deployment steps I need to perform."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# ROLE: Senior Software Architect, Full-Stack Developer & UI/UX Engineer\n\nAct as a Senior Software Architect and Full-Stack Developer. Your task is to upgrade my existing GitHub project into a professional, production-quality Smart Campus Attendance Management System.\n\n**Existing GitHub Repository:**\nhttps://github.com/pankajadhikari820/mini-attendence.git\n\nFirst, thoroughly inspect the entire existing codebase, including frontend, backend, Firebase configuration, Firestore collections, authentication, security rules, user roles, and existing attendance workflows.\n\nDo not blindly rewrite the project. Understand the existing architecture before making changes.\n\n# 1. CRITICAL: Remove Late Attendance Completely\n\nCompletely eliminate the Late Attendance feature from the entire project.\n\nThe system must support ONLY these three attendance statuses:\n\n- PRESENT\n- ABSENT\n- LEAVE\n\nRequirements:\n- Remove LATE status from all frontend components.\n- Remove late attendance calculations and conditions.\n- Remove late-related dashboard statistics.\n- Remove late-related reports, filters, labels, and notifications.\n- Update Firestore-related logic and validation wherever necessary.\n- Ensure no legacy code can generate or display a LATE status.\n\nDo not introduce any additional attendance status.\n\n# 2. Digital Student ID Card\n\nCreate a professional digital student identity card.\n\nFeatures:\n- Student name and photograph.\n- Unique student ID / enrollment number.\n- Department and course.\n- Semester and section.\n- College name and logo.\n- Unique QR code linked to the student\'s authenticated identity.\n- Downloadable digital ID card in PDF format.\n\nEnsure the ID QR code cannot independently authorize attendance.\n\n# 3. Advanced Attendance Analytics\n\nCreate a modern analytics dashboard with:\n\n- Weekly and monthly attendance graphs.\n- Overall attendance percentage.\n- Subject-wise attendance.\n- PRESENT, ABSENT, and LEAVE statistics.\n- Attendance trends.\n- Department-wise and class-wise analytics.\n- Date range filters.\n- Interactive charts and downloadable reports.\n\n# 4. Low Attendance Alert System\n\nImplement an automated attendance monitoring system.\n\nRequirements:\n- Set 75% as the default attendance threshold.\n- Automatically identify students whose attendance percentage falls below 75%.\n- Notify students and authorized faculty.\n- Display low-attendance warnings on dashboards.\n- Show subject-wise attendance shortage.\n- Avoid duplicate notifications.\n- Allow authorized administrators to configure the threshold.\n\n# 5. Parent Portal\n\nCreate a separate, secure parent dashboard.\n\nFeatures:\n- Parent login.\n- Link parent accounts with verified student accounts.\n- View child\'s attendance percentage.\n- View subject-wise attendance.\n- View attendance history.\n- View leave applications and approval status.\n- Download attendance reports.\n- Receive low-attendance alerts.\n\nSecurity:\n- Parents must only access their linked child\'s information.\n- Parent accounts must not have permission to modify attendance.\n- Implement proper account verification and access control.\n\n# 6. Subject-wise and Classroom Attendance\n\nUpgrade the existing attendance workflow.\n\nFeatures:\n- Department management.\n- Course management.\n- Semester management.\n- Section management.\n- Classroom management.\n- Subject management.\n- Faculty-to-subject assignment.\n- Faculty-to-class assignment.\n- Subject-specific attendance sessions.\n- Student enrollment in subjects.\n\nFaculty should only be able to create and manage attendance sessions for their assigned classes and subjects.\n\n# 7. Complete Audit Log System\n\nImplement immutable audit logs for important system actions.\n\nTrack:\n- Attendance creation.\n- Attendance modification.\n- Attendance correction.\n- Leave approval and rejection.\n- Student account changes.\n- Faculty actions.\n- Role and permission changes.\n- Administrative actions.\n\nEach audit record should include:\n- User ID.\n- User role.\n- Action performed.\n- Target record.\n- Timestamp.\n- Relevant metadata.\n\nPrevent normal users and administrators from editing or deleting audit history. Use trusted backend operations and appropriate security rules.\n\n# 8. Smart Timetable Integration\n\nBuild a timetable management system.\n\nFeatures:\n- Weekly timetable.\n- Subject scheduling.\n- Faculty scheduling.\n- Classroom allocation.\n- Automatic attendance session creation.\n- Session start and end times.\n- Conflict detection.\n- Automatic reminders for faculty.\n- Prevent duplicate sessions.\n\nAttendance sessions must follow the configured timetable and authorized faculty assignments.\n\n# 9. Student Performance Dashboard\n\nCreate a personalized student dashboard.\n\nDisplay:\n- Overall attendance percentage.\n- Subject-wise attendance.\n- Weekly attendance trends.\n- Monthly progress.\n- Attendance shortage warnings.\n- Recent attendance history.\n- Leave history.\n- Upcoming timetable.\n\nUse clean charts, statistics, and modern visual components.\n\n# 10. Professional Excel/PDF Reporting\n\nImplement report generation for:\n\n- Individual students.\n- Individual subjects.\n- Classes and sections.\n- Departments.\n- Monthly attendance.\n- Date-range attendance.\n- PRESENT, ABSENT, and LEAVE summaries.\n\nSupport:\n- Excel export.\n- Professional PDF export.\n- College branding.\n- Generation timestamps.\n- Proper report formatting.\n\n# 11. Role-Based Access Control\n\nMaintain and strengthen the existing role hierarchy.\n\nSupport:\n- Super Admin.\n- Organization Admin.\n- Manager / Faculty.\n- Student.\n- Parent.\n\nEnforce permissions at the backend and Firestore Security Rules level.\n\nDo not rely only on hiding frontend buttons.\n\n# 12. UI/UX Modernization\n\nUpgrade the complete application interface to a premium SaaS-level design.\n\nDesign direction:\n- Modern professional dashboard.\n- Clean typography.\n- Consistent spacing.\n- Responsive layouts.\n- Professional sidebar navigation.\n- Attractive analytics cards.\n- Interactive graphs.\n- Smooth but subtle animations.\n- Consistent design system.\n- Light and dark modes.\n- Mobile-friendly student and parent dashboards.\n\nAvoid excessive gradients, unnecessary animations, and cluttered layouts.\n\nMaintain the existing college identity and branding wherever available.\n\n# 13. Firebase Architecture and Security\n\nPreserve the existing Firebase architecture wherever practical.\n\nReview and improve:\n- Firebase Authentication.\n- Cloud Firestore.\n- Firestore Security Rules.\n- Firebase Storage.\n- Cloud Functions, if available and required.\n- Data validation.\n- Role-based permissions.\n- Database indexes.\n- Secure API operations.\n\nImportant:\n- Never expose Firebase Admin credentials or private API keys.\n- Never trust client-side role information.\n- Validate attendance submissions on trusted backend infrastructure.\n- Prevent duplicate attendance records.\n- Prevent unauthorized attendance modifications.\n- Protect student and parent information.\n- Follow the principle of least privilege.\n\nDo not assume Firebase Spark Plan supports every backend capability. Identify paid-plan requirements, external service dependencies, and deployment requirements before integrating them.\n\n# 14. Implementation Rules\n\nFollow these instructions strictly:\n\n1. First inspect the complete existing project.\n2. Identify existing features and preserve working functionality.\n3. Remove Late Attendance completely.\n4. Prepare a clear implementation plan.\n5. Upgrade the existing application instead of building an unrelated new project.\n6. Reuse existing components and services wherever appropriate.\n7. Maintain existing database compatibility and migrate legacy data safely.\n8. Implement real functionality, not just UI mockups.\n9. Do not use fake data for production features.\n10. Do not leave buttons or forms non-functional.\n11. Add proper loading, success, and error states.\n12. Validate all user inputs.\n13. Handle edge cases and network failures.\n14. Test authentication, role permissions, attendance workflows, and security rules.\n15. Fix build errors and runtime errors.\n16. Update README documentation and deployment instructions.\n\n# 15. STRICT EXCLUSIONS\n\nDo NOT implement:\n- Face Recognition.\n- Facial Verification.\n- Biometric Authentication.\n- Face Enrollment.\n- Facial Embeddings.\n- Liveness Detection.\n- Biometric Data Storage.\n\nKeep the attendance process based on the existing QR, GPS, authentication, and authorized verification mechanisms.\n\n# FINAL OBJECTIVE\n\nTransform the existing project into a complete, secure, modern and scalable:\n\nSMART CAMPUS ATTENDANCE MANAGEMENT SYSTEM\n\nThe final application should feel like a professional campus management SaaS product rather than a basic college QR attendance project.\n\nPrioritize functionality, security, usability, database integrity, and maintainable architecture over unnecessary visual effects.\n\nDo not stop after generating a plan or redesigning the UI. Implement the features in the existing codebase, verify them, and provide a final summary of:\n\n- Completed features.\n- Modified files.\n- Database changes.\n- Security improvements.\n- Deployment requirements.\n- Firebase plan requirements.\n- Any features that still require external services.\n\nEnsure all three attendance statuses remain strictly PRESENT, ABSENT, and LEAVE."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# COMPLETE PROJECT UPDATE \342\200\223 SMART ATTENDANCE MANAGEMENT SYSTEM\n\nAct as a senior full-stack developer and Firebase architect. First inspect my entire existing project, understand its current architecture, database schema, authentication, role hierarchy, components, and workflows. Then update and fix the project without unnecessarily rebuilding or breaking existing functionality.\n\n## 1. IMPORTANT: REMOVE UNWANTED FEATURES\n\nCompletely remove the following:\n\n- Late attendance status and all related functionality.\n- Face Recognition and Facial Verification.\n- Biometric Authentication.\n- Face Enrollment, Facial Embeddings, and Liveness Detection.\n- Any facial or biometric data storage.\n\nThe attendance system must use ONLY these three statuses:\n\n- PRESENT\n- ABSENT\n- LEAVE\n\nRemove all references to LATE from frontend, backend, Firebase validation, reports, analytics, filters, statistics, notifications, and legacy data handling.\n\nDo not introduce any facial or biometric functionality.\n\n## 2. FIX LIVE ATTENDANCE SESSION ISSUE\n\nCurrently, the student dashboard displays:\n\n\"No active session right now. Ask your organizer to start a live attendance session. This screen updates automatically. Check again.\"\n\nThis functionality is not working correctly.\n\nInvestigate and fix the complete session workflow, not just the UI.\n\nExpected workflow:\n\n1. Authorized Teacher/Manager logs in.\n2. Selects assigned class, section, semester, and subject.\n3. Clicks Start Attendance.\n4. A new session is created correctly in Firebase.\n5. Session status becomes OPEN.\n6. Students assigned to that class and subject receive the active session in real time.\n7. Students can scan the dynamic QR code.\n8. QR token and GPS location are validated.\n9. Valid attendance is submitted as PRESENT.\n10. Teacher can see attendance updates in real time.\n11. Teacher can close the session.\n12. Students can no longer submit attendance after session closure or expiry.\n\nTechnical requirements:\n\n- Inspect existing Firestore collections and field names before making changes.\n- Verify session and sessionSecrets creation and their relationship.\n- Fix incorrect Firestore queries, document references, listeners, and collection paths.\n- Use correct onSnapshot() listeners for real-time updates.\n- Verify Firebase Authentication and Firestore Security Rules.\n- Check role-based permissions and organization/class assignments.\n- Handle loading, empty, permission-denied, network-error, and expired-session states separately.\n- Ensure the Check Again button actually refreshes/retries the required data.\n- Prevent duplicate attendance submissions.\n- Ensure listeners unsubscribe properly.\n\nNever solve this issue by adding mock sessions, dummy data, or public Firestore permissions.\n\n## 3. MULTIPLE TEACHERS AND SUBJECT-WISE ATTENDANCE\n\nThe system must support multiple teachers taking attendance for different lectures on the same day.\n\nExample:\n\nTeacher 1:\n- Subject: Mathematics\n- Class: BCA AI & DS\n- Semester: 2\n- Section: C\n- Time: 09:00 AM \342\200\223 10:00 AM\n\nTeacher 2:\n- Subject: Programming\n- Class: BCA AI & DS\n- Semester: 2\n- Section: C\n- Time: 10:00 AM \342\200\223 11:00 AM\n\nBoth teachers must be able to independently start and manage their attendance sessions.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Every subject must have its own independent attendance session.\n- Every session must have a unique sessionId.\n- Each session must be associated with its assigned teacher, subject, class, semester, and section.\n- One teacher\'s attendance session must not overwrite another teacher\'s session.\n- Students must see only the sessions relevant to their assigned class and section.\n- Attendance for Mathematics must not affect Programming attendance.\n- Multiple sessions must be supported without conflicts.\n\n## 4. TEACHER DASHBOARD\n\nCreate a proper Teacher Dashboard with:\n\n- My Classes\n- My Assigned Subjects\n- My Lectures\n- Start Attendance\n- Active Sessions\n- Today\'s Attendance\n- Attendance History\n- Subject-wise Attendance Reports\n\nTeacher workflow:\n\n1. Login.\n2. View assigned classes and subjects.\n3. Select a subject and class.\n4. Start a live attendance session.\n5. Display a dynamic QR code.\n6. View students joining in real time.\n7. View PRESENT, ABSENT, and LEAVE counts.\n8. Close the session.\n9. Download attendance reports.\n\nTeachers must only access classes and subjects assigned to them.\n\n## 5. STUDENT DASHBOARD\n\nCreate a clear and functional student dashboard.\n\nDisplay:\n\n- Student Profile\n- Digital Student ID Card with QR\n- Today\'s Lectures\n- Active Attendance Sessions\n- Subject-wise Attendance Percentage\n- Overall Attendance Percentage\n- Attendance History\n- Timetable\n\nToday\'s Lectures should show:\n\n- Subject Name\n- Teacher Name\n- Lecture Time\n- Attendance Status\n- Active Session Indicator\n\nAttendance states:\n\n- PRESENT\n- ABSENT\n- LEAVE\n- NOT STARTED\n\nWhen no session is active, show a proper empty state.\n\nWhen a teacher starts an eligible session, update the student dashboard automatically without requiring a manual page refresh.\n\nStudents must only be able to mark attendance for their own account and eligible active sessions.\n\n## 6. ROLE-BASED ACCESS CONTROL\n\nMaintain and carefully extend the existing role hierarchy.\n\nRequired roles:\n\n- Super Admin\n- Organization Admin\n- Manager / Faculty / Teacher\n- Student\n- Parent\n\nPermissions:\n\nSuper Admin:\n- Manage organizations and system-level settings.\n\nOrganization Admin:\n- Manage students, teachers, classes, subjects, sections, and timetables.\n- Assign teachers to subjects and classes.\n- View organization-wide reports.\n\nManager / Faculty / Teacher:\n- Access only assigned classes and subjects.\n- Start and close attendance sessions.\n- View and export attendance records for assigned lectures.\n\nStudent:\n- View personal attendance.\n- Scan QR codes for eligible sessions.\n- View timetable and attendance reports.\n\nParent:\n- View linked student\'s attendance and low-attendance alerts.\n- No permission to modify attendance.\n\nDo not break the existing authentication or role structure. Map these roles to the existing system wherever possible.\n\n## 7. FIREBASE DATABASE STRUCTURE\n\nInspect the existing Firestore schema first.\n\nReuse existing collections and fields wherever possible. Only introduce new collections or fields when genuinely necessary.\n\nThe system must logically support:\n\n- organizations\n- users\n- students\n- teachers\n- classes\n- sections\n- subjects\n- facultyAssignments\n- timetables\n- sessions\n- sessionSecrets\n- attendance\n- notifications\n- auditLogs\n\nEvery attendance record must be associated with the correct:\n\n- organizationId\n- studentId\n- teacherId\n- subjectId\n- classId\n- sectionId\n- sessionId\n- date\n- status\n- timestamp\n\nUse unique session IDs and prevent duplicate student attendance within the same session.\n\nEnsure appropriate Firestore indexes and secure access rules.\n\n## 8. ATTENDANCE ANALYTICS\n\nProvide:\n\n- Overall attendance percentage.\n- Subject-wise attendance percentage.\n- Student-wise attendance reports.\n- Class-wise attendance reports.\n- Daily and monthly attendance statistics.\n- PRESENT, ABSENT, and LEAVE counts.\n- Students below 75% attendance.\n- Low-attendance notifications.\n\nUse the correct attendance denominator and avoid counting multiple lectures as a single attendance record.\n\n## 9. ADDITIONAL FEATURES\n\nImplement or improve:\n\n- Digital Student ID Card with QR.\n- Parent Portal.\n- 75% attendance warning system.\n- Subject-wise and classroom-wise attendance.\n- Smart timetable integration.\n- Attendance audit logs.\n- Excel and PDF report exports.\n- Notifications for low attendance.\n- Responsive mobile-friendly PWA interface.\n\n## 10. SECURITY AND VALIDATION\n\n- Use secure Firebase Authentication.\n- Apply proper role-based Firestore Security Rules.\n- Never expose session secrets or private QR validation data to unauthorized users.\n- Validate QR tokens on the trusted side of the system.\n- Verify GPS location using the existing configured geofence.\n- Prevent fake, expired, reused, or invalid QR submissions.\n- Prevent duplicate attendance records.\n- Do not allow students to edit their attendance status.\n- Log important attendance actions in audit logs.\n- Do not use unrestricted Firestore read/write rules.\n\n## 11. UI/UX IMPROVEMENT\n\nModernize the existing UI while preserving the project\'s identity and working features.\n\nDesign requirements:\n\n- Clean, modern, professional dashboard.\n- Responsive layout for desktop, tablet, and mobile.\n- Clear navigation for every role.\n- Proper loading skeletons.\n- Meaningful empty states.\n- Clear success and error messages.\n- Real-time dashboard updates.\n- Easy-to-read attendance charts and tables.\n- Consistent buttons, spacing, typography, and colors.\n\n## 12. FIREBASE SPARK PLAN COMPATIBILITY\n\nKeep the project compatible with the Firebase Spark (free) plan wherever possible.\n\nAvoid introducing unnecessary paid services or paid APIs.\n\nIf any proposed feature requires a paid Firebase service or third-party subscription, identify it clearly and provide a free alternative where possible.\n\n## 13. TESTING REQUIREMENTS\n\nBefore considering the project complete, test these scenarios:\n\n1. Teacher 1 starts a Mathematics attendance session.\n2. Teacher 2 starts a Programming attendance session on the same day.\n3. Both sessions work independently.\n4. Students see only eligible sessions.\n5. Attendance in Mathematics does not overwrite Programming attendance.\n6. Valid QR and GPS verification marks PRESENT.\n7. Invalid or expired QR codes are rejected.\n8. Closed sessions reject new attendance submissions.\n9. Duplicate attendance is prevented.\n10. Teachers cannot access unassigned classes.\n11. Students cannot modify their own attendance.\n12. Parents can only view linked student information.\n13. Dashboard updates automatically when sessions start or close.\n14. Network disconnection and reconnection are handled correctly.\n15. All reports and analytics use only PRESENT, ABSENT, and LEAVE.\n\n## FINAL INSTRUCTIONS\n\n- First inspect the complete existing codebase.\n- Understand the existing Firebase schema and role hierarchy.\n- Identify the actual cause of the live session issue.\n- Fix the root cause instead of applying temporary UI workarounds.\n- Implement multiple teachers and independent subject-wise sessions.\n- Remove Late and all facial/biometric features completely.\n- Keep QR + GPS-based attendance.\n- Preserve existing working functionality.\n- Do not create unnecessary duplicate components or collections.\n- Do not use mock data in production workflows.\n- Do not weaken security rules to make features work.\n- Test the complete workflow before finishing.\n\nAt the end, provide a clear summary of:\n1. Files modified.\n2. Features implemented.\n3. Live session issue and its root cause.\n4. Firebase schema or security rule changes.\n5. Any required Firestore indexes.\n6. Tests performed.\n7. Any remaining limitations.\n\n**Most important: Make the entire attendance workflow actually functional with real Firebase data, multiple teachers, multiple subjects, and real-time updates.**"
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "COMPLETE PROJECT UPDATE \342\200\223 SMART ATTENDANCE MANAGEMENT SYSTEM\nAct as a senior full-stack developer and Firebase architect. First inspect my entire existing project, understand its current architecture, database schema, authentication, role hierarchy, components, and workflows. Then update and fix the project without unnecessarily rebuilding or breaking existing functionality.\n1. IMPORTANT: REMOVE UNWANTED FEATURES\nCompletely remove the following:\nLate attendance status and all related functionality.\nFace Recognition and Facial Verification.\nBiometric Authentication.\nFace Enrollment, Facial Embeddings, and Liveness Detection.\nAny facial or biometric data storage.\nThe attendance system must use ONLY these three statuses:\nPRESENT\nABSENT\nLEAVE\nRemove all references to LATE from frontend, backend, Firebase validation, reports, analytics, filters, statistics, notifications, and legacy data handling.\nDo not introduce any facial or biometric functionality.\n2. FIX LIVE ATTENDANCE SESSION ISSUE\nCurrently, the student dashboard displays:\n\"No active session right now. Ask your organizer to start a live attendance session. This screen updates automatically. Check again.\"\nThis functionality is not working correctly.\nInvestigate and fix the complete session workflow, not just the UI.\nExpected workflow:\nAuthorized Teacher/Manager logs in.\nSelects assigned class, section, semester, and subject.\nClicks Start Attendance.\nA new session is created correctly in Firebase.\nSession status becomes OPEN.\nStudents assigned to that class and subject receive the active session in real time.\nStudents can scan the dynamic QR code.\nQR token and GPS location are validated.\nValid attendance is submitted as PRESENT.\nTeacher can see attendance updates in real time.\nTeacher can close the session.\nStudents can no longer submit attendance after session closure or expiry.\nTechnical requirements:\nInspect existing Firestore collections and field name